.TH DOTS 1 "2026-08-30" "dots" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dots \- orchestration CLI for the dots dotfiles repo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dots
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dots
is the machine-side entry point for the repository, installed as
.B ~/bin/dots
by stow (source:
.IR common/bin/bin/dots ).
It delegates every existing operation one-for-one to the repo's
.I scripts/*.sh
and the
.B docs/man
Makefile, mirrors the two read-only
.BR just (1)
recipes
.RB ( status ,
.BR list-packages ),
and adds two composed commands,
.B sync
and
.BR commit .
.PP
It is strictly an orchestrator: stow invocation, age encryption, and git
operations are sequenced and gated here, never re-implemented. The only state
it manages itself is the interactive plan built by
.B dots commit
(below). All human output goes to standard error; standard output is reserved
for command payloads.
.PP
The repo checkout is resolved from the path of the
.B dots
binary itself (symlinks are followed without
.BR readlink -f ,
so the stowed
.B ~/bin/dots
and a checkout invocation
.IR common/bin/bin/dots " behave identically), and"
.B DOTS_DIR
is exported to that tree before delegating, so the scripts act on the same
checkout the command was resolved from rather than the default
.IR ~/.dots .
Like
.BR runtimes (1),
it is portable bash, written to run under macOS
.I /bin/bash
3.2 as well as any interactive bash →
no associative arrays, no
.BR mapfile ,
no
.BR read - i ,
 BusyBox-safe coreutils only.
.SH COMMANDS
Delegating commands run the matching script with
.B exec
and its exit status; their options and diagnostics are those of the target
script.
.TP
.B setup
.I scripts/setup.sh
\(em full bootstrap (deps, decrypt, stow).
.TP
.B restow
.I scripts/setup.sh\ --restow
.TP
.B adopt
.I scripts/setup.sh\ --adopt
(wholesale adopt; for per-file adoption use
.BR "dots commit" )
.TP
.B unstow
.I scripts/setup.sh\ --unstow
.TP
.B verify
.RI [ options ]
.I scripts/verify.sh
\(em deployment health check. See
.BR dots-verify (1).
.TP
.B encrypt
.I plaintext-file
.RI [ output.age ]
.I scripts/encrypt.sh
\(em write the dual age artifacts. See
.BR dots-encrypt (1).
.TP
.B decrypt
.I scripts/decrypt.sh
\(em materialize secrets locally. See
.BR dots-decrypt (1).
.TP
.B status
Stow dry-run
.RB ( stow \ -n )
for the stow layers this host would actually receive →
.I common ,
the platform layer, and the applicable
.I alpine
/
.I wsl
layers, detected as
.I scripts/setup.sh
does. The other platforms' packages are skipped: they collide by design with
the currently-stowed equivalents
.RB ( .bash_profile
et al. are per-platform), so probing them here is always noise. One
.B ok
or
.B CONFLICT
line per package, with stow's conflict output indented under the latter.
Exits non-zero if stow is not installed or any package has an error.
.B list-packages
by contrast stays a full inventory.
.TP
.B list-packages
Prints every stow package as
.IB base / pkg
(mirrors
.BR "just list-packages" ).
.TP
.BI packages " <cmd>"
Passthrough to the wishlist CLI
.RB ( packages ( 1 )) \(em
.B status ,
.B install ,
.BR update ,
.B refresh ,
.B add \(em
exec'd from the checkout
.B dots
was resolved from.
.TP
.B man-check | man-install | man-uninstall
.B make -C docs/man
with the same target; see
.BR dots-setup (1)
for the manpage workflow.
.TP
.B sync
Update the deployment in one step:
.B git pull --ff-only
(with
.B --autostash
when the working tree is dirty), then
.I scripts/setup.sh\ --restow ,
then
.IR scripts/verify.sh .
A restow or verify failure is reported but does not stop the later steps;
the command exits non-zero if either failed. A failed pull aborts before
anything is restowed, leaving the checkout and deployment untouched.
.TP
.B commit
.RB [ -m \ <msg> ]
The composed, interactive command: survey, plan, review, commit. Never
pushes. See
.B THE COMMIT WORKFLOW
below.
.TP
.B -h | --help | help
Usage summary.
.SH THE COMMIT WORKFLOW
.B dots commit
turns "I edited my configs, now what?" into one guided commit. It never
pushes, and plaintext secrets can never pass through it.
.SS Survey and plan
The tree is surveyed read-only:
.IP \(bu 3
.B git status
(untracked and worktree-modified paths; ignored paths via
.BR --ignored=matching )
minus editor/OS/cache noise;
.IP \(bu 3
plaintext files inside
.I secrets/
that are not yet
.B .age
artifacts;
.IP \(bu 3
files in
.I ~/.ssh
and
.I ~/.config/tokens
without a committed
.IB X .age
.IB X .phrase.age
artifact pair;
.IP \(bu 3
stray, managed-looking files in
.I $HOME
that fit into an existing stow package (searched only inside package anchor
directories, never across all of
.BR $HOME ).
.IP \(bu 3
Paths already staged in the index are reported informational; they are
included in the commit but never re-added or reset.
.PP
The resulting plan is printed in groups. Where no terminal is available
(piped input, no controlling TTY), the plan is printed and the command exits
2 without changing anything.
.SS Confirmation
Each item is prompted
.BR y / n / a / q :
a accepts the remainder of its group, q aborts. Aborting →
and any later failure →
restores the index to its pre-command state by resetting exactly the paths
this command staged; pre-existing staged entries are preserved, and nothing
is ever committed after an abort.
.SS Execute
Accepted plan items are carried out before staging, so everything lands in a
single commit:
.IP \(bu 3
encrypt items run
.B "scripts/encrypt.sh --no-commit"
(equivalently
.BR dots-encrypt (1)
with the auto-commit skipped), writing both artifacts; the artifact pair is
then staged as part of the commit.
.IP \(bu 3
adopt items are copied into the package
.RB ( cp -p ),
then one scoped
.B stow
per affected package is offered so the symlinks follow. The wholesale
.B "dots adopt"
remains available for machines that want everything adopted at once.
.SS Plaintext gate
After staging, the staged index itself is checked → not the worktree:
.IP \(bu 3
every file staged under
.I secrets/
must be a
.B .age
artifact whose first line is the age magic
.RB ( age-encryption.org/v1 )
and which has a
.I .phrase.age
twin (the repo's dual-artifact invariant), with only
.I recipients.txt
and trivial dotfiles exempt;
.IP \(bu 3
no file anywhere in the staged tree may carry a plaintext-secret basename
.RB ( id_* ,
.B *.pem ,
.B *.key ,
.BR *.secret ,
.B .env*
excepting
.B *.pub
and
.BR .env.example ).
.PP
A violation is a hard refusal: the message lists the offenders and the
command exits 1 with the index left as staged, so nothing is lost by
correcting
.RB ( git " reset " -- <path>).
.SS Review, message, commit
.B git diff --cached --stat
is shown, followed by an optional pagered full diff. After confirmation, a
commit message is generated from the accepted items → subject
.RB " configs: <packages>; +N secrets (<subdirs>) "
with a bulleted body → written to a temporary file. The default flow
seeds the editor with it via
.B git commit -t
(so the default is a genuine starting buffer; an emptied buffer aborts with
the index intact); declining the edit commits with
.B git commit -F .
Alternatively
.B "dots commit -m <msg>"
bypasses the plan-confirmed message entirely (the survey, gates, and review
still run).
.PP
On success a
.B git log -1 --stat
summary is printed and a reminder that nothing was pushed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -m ", " --message " <msg>"
.B dots commit
only: commit with the given message instead of the generated one. Survey,
encryption gate, and diff review are unchanged.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Requested operation completed. For
.BR commit :
committed, or the working tree was clean.
.TP
.B 1
Delegated script failed; unknown command; for
.BR commit :
any preflight failure (merge conflicts, aborted detached HEAD), abort,
plaintext-gate refusal, or failed commit (e.g. the pre-commit shellcheck
hook); the index is preserved in that state for
.BR commit .
.TP
.B 2
.B commit
run without a TTY: the plan was printed, nothing was changed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DOTS_DIR
Exported as the resolved repo root before delegating, so
.I scripts/setup.sh
acts on the same checkout (overrides its
.I ~/.dots
default for the child process only).
.TP
.B EDITOR
Used by
.B git commit -t
in
.B dots commit
(git's own default applies when unset, ultimately
.BR vi ).
.TP
.B DOTS_NO_COMMIT
Passed through to
.BR dots-encrypt (1)
semantics via the
.B --no-commit
flag
.B dots commit
supplies itself.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I common/bin/bin/dots
The shim; stowed to
.IR ~/bin/dots .
.TP
.I scripts/setup.sh ", " scripts/verify.sh ", " scripts/encrypt.sh ", " scripts/decrypt.sh
The delegation targets; see
.BR dots-setup (1),
.BR dots-verify (1),
.BR dots-encrypt (1),
.BR dots-decrypt (1).
.TP
.I common/bin/bin/packages
The target of the
.B packages
passthrough; see
.BR packages (1).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
All
.B dots
messages are prefixed
.B ==>
and go to standard error
.RB ( info / WARNING / ERROR ) .
Delegated scripts print their own output. Notable
.B dots
messages:
.TP
.B "plaintext secret-named file in the checkout (not staged, not committed): <path>"
Surveyed a file that looks like plaintext secret material; it was excluded
from the plan. Staging it manually will trip the gate.
.TP
.B "refusing to commit \(em stage only age ciphertext"
The staged-content gate fired; offending paths are listed above the message.
.TP
.B "committed \(em nothing was pushed (dots never pushes)"
End-of-run confirmation.
.SH EXAMPLES
Guided commit of config edits and any newly appeared secrets:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&dots commit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scripted commit with a fixed message (gates still apply):
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&dots commit -m "vim: tighten clipboard guard"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pull, re-stow, and verify on a blessed machine:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&dots sync
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pre-flight conflict check before a restow:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&dots status
.fi
.RE
.PP
Through
.BR just (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
just dots commit
just dots sync
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dots-setup (1),
.BR dots-encrypt (1),
.BR dots-decrypt (1),
.BR dots-verify (1),
.BR runtimes (1),
.BR packages (1),
.BR stow (1),
.BR just (1)